1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:07,467
Every month, someone opens a giant spreadsheet, filters it, copies numbers into a new file, adds totals, and makes it look nice.

2
00:00:07,467 --> 00:00:08,821
It eats a whole morning.

3
00:00:08,821 --> 00:00:12,260
This report was made by a Python script, in under one second.

4
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:15,644
Three sheets, a totals row, bold headers, and clean numbers.

5
00:00:15,644 --> 00:00:16,941
Today, you'll build it.

6
00:00:17,641 --> 00:00:21,550
We'll start from the clean orders file we made in the last episode.

7
00:00:21,550 --> 00:00:27,033
If you missed it, no problem, any table with dates, categories and numbers works the same way.

8
00:00:27,033 --> 00:00:33,159
And I'll show you the three mistakes that break almost every first report, with the real error on screen.

9
00:00:33,859 --> 00:00:34,834
Here's the plan.

10
00:00:34,834 --> 00:00:35,687
Load the data.

11
00:00:35,687 --> 00:00:37,698
Add the columns the report needs.

12
00:00:37,698 --> 00:00:39,830
Summarize it, by city and by month.

13
00:00:39,830 --> 00:00:43,060
Write everything to one Excel file with three sheets.

14
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:45,985
And finally, make it look like a person made it.

15
00:00:46,685 --> 00:00:49,364
You need Python, and two free libraries.

16
00:00:49,364 --> 00:00:54,922
Pandas does the math, and openpyxl is the engine that reads and writes Excel files.

17
00:00:54,922 --> 00:00:58,471
Open a terminal and type pip install pandas openpyxl.

18
00:00:58,471 --> 00:01:05,168
Then put orders clean dot c s v in a folder, and create a new file next to it called report dot p y.

19
00:01:05,868 --> 00:01:06,422
Step one.

20
00:01:06,422 --> 00:01:09,868
Import pandas as p d, and read the file with read c s v.

21
00:01:09,868 --> 00:01:11,283
Notice this extra part.

22
00:01:11,283 --> 00:01:13,498
Parse dates equals a list with Date.

23
00:01:13,498 --> 00:01:17,744
It tells pandas that the Date column holds real dates, not just text.

24
00:01:17,744 --> 00:01:20,452
Remember that, it's the first mistake later.

25
00:01:20,452 --> 00:01:23,221
Then print the shape and the first four rows.

26
00:01:23,921 --> 00:01:24,348
Run it.

27
00:01:24,348 --> 00:01:27,520
Eight hundred and ninety two rows and eight columns.

28
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:31,730
Order I D, date, customer, city, product, quantity, price and status.

29
00:01:31,730 --> 00:01:32,706
Look at row two.

30
00:01:32,706 --> 00:01:34,231
That order was cancelled.

31
00:01:34,231 --> 00:01:37,220
We don't want cancelled orders in a sales report.

32
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:38,455
Step two.

33
00:01:38,455 --> 00:01:42,318
First, keep only the rows where status is not equal to cancelled.

34
00:01:42,318 --> 00:01:47,489
Inside the square brackets is a test, and pandas keeps the rows where the test is true.

35
00:01:47,489 --> 00:01:52,303
Then a new column, Revenue, which is quantity times price, for every row at once.

36
00:01:52,303 --> 00:01:56,641
And Month, using dot d t dot s t r f time, with percent Y dash percent m.

37
00:01:56,641 --> 00:02:00,563
That turns a date into text like twenty twenty six dash zero four.

38
00:02:01,264 --> 00:02:03,028
And here's mistake number one.

39
00:02:03,028 --> 00:02:07,673
If you forget parse dates when you read the file, the Date column is just text.

40
00:02:07,673 --> 00:02:10,672
The moment you use dot d t, pandas stops with this.

41
00:02:10,672 --> 00:02:11,613
Attribute error.

42
00:02:11,613 --> 00:02:14,906
Can only use dot d t accessor with datetime like values.

43
00:02:14,906 --> 00:02:17,375
In plain English, that column isn't dates.

44
00:02:17,375 --> 00:02:19,433
The fix is the one we already have.

45
00:02:19,433 --> 00:02:21,198
Add parse dates to read c s v.

46
00:02:21,898 --> 00:02:23,584
Step three, the first summary.

47
00:02:23,584 --> 00:02:26,450
Group by City, take the Revenue column, and sum it.

48
00:02:26,450 --> 00:02:28,979
Group by works like sorting cards into piles.

49
00:02:28,979 --> 00:02:32,969
Every row goes to the pile of its city, and then each pile is added up.

50
00:02:32,969 --> 00:02:39,826
Here it is, running for real, on all eight hundred and twenty five orders that are left after removing the cancelled ones.

51
00:02:40,526 --> 00:02:45,065
We add sort values, ascending equals false, so the biggest city comes first.

52
00:02:45,065 --> 00:02:45,483
Run it.

53
00:02:45,483 --> 00:02:49,185
Austin on top, with almost one hundred and sixty two thousand.

54
00:02:49,185 --> 00:02:50,559
Denver near the bottom.

55
00:02:50,559 --> 00:02:55,456
And Unknown, those are orders with an empty city that we kept in the last episode.

56
00:02:55,456 --> 00:02:58,263
Honest data shows them, instead of hiding them.

57
00:02:58,963 --> 00:03:02,519
Step four, a pivot table, exactly like the one in Excel.

58
00:03:02,519 --> 00:03:05,250
Index equals Month makes one row per month.

59
00:03:05,250 --> 00:03:08,172
Columns equals City makes one column per city.

60
00:03:08,172 --> 00:03:11,729
Values is Revenue, and agg func equals sum adds them up.

61
00:03:11,729 --> 00:03:17,254
Fill value equals zero puts a zero where a city had no sales, instead of an empty cell.

62
00:03:17,954 --> 00:03:19,232
And here's the result.

63
00:03:19,232 --> 00:03:25,740
Ten months down the side, seven cities across the top, and every cell is the real total for that month and city.

64
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:27,367
I rounded it for the screen.

65
00:03:27,367 --> 00:03:29,923
Building this by hand in Excel takes clicks.

66
00:03:29,923 --> 00:03:34,048
Here it's two lines, and it updates itself every time the data changes.

67
00:03:34,748 --> 00:03:36,094
Now we write to Excel.

68
00:03:36,094 --> 00:03:37,990
And this is mistake number two.

69
00:03:37,990 --> 00:03:45,023
It feels natural to call to excel twice, one sheet for the summary, one for the data, both into report dot x l s x.

70
00:03:45,023 --> 00:03:46,736
Run it, and list the sheets.

71
00:03:46,736 --> 00:03:47,347
Only Data.

72
00:03:47,347 --> 00:03:52,118
The second call created a brand new file, and silently replaced the first one.

73
00:03:52,118 --> 00:03:53,402
Your summary is gone.

74
00:03:54,102 --> 00:03:55,548
Here's the real output.

75
00:03:55,548 --> 00:03:56,993
One sheet, called Data.

76
00:03:56,993 --> 00:03:58,313
No error, no warning.

77
00:03:58,313 --> 00:04:00,639
That's what makes this one so sneaky.

78
00:04:01,339 --> 00:04:02,853
The fix is Excel Writer.

79
00:04:02,853 --> 00:04:06,450
With p d dot Excel Writer, report dot x l s x, as writer.

80
00:04:06,450 --> 00:04:10,677
Everything indented under that line writes into the same open file.

81
00:04:10,677 --> 00:04:16,986
Then call to excel three times, passing writer instead of a file name, each with its own sheet name.

82
00:04:16,986 --> 00:04:20,078
The file is saved once, when the with block ends.

83
00:04:20,778 --> 00:04:21,587
Open the file.

84
00:04:21,587 --> 00:04:23,031
Three tabs at the bottom.

85
00:04:23,031 --> 00:04:24,648
By city, By month, and Data.

86
00:04:24,648 --> 00:04:25,168
It works.

87
00:04:25,168 --> 00:04:26,035
But look at it.

88
00:04:26,035 --> 00:04:28,230
Every column is the same narrow width.

89
00:04:28,230 --> 00:04:32,446
In the Data sheet, the dates are just hash signs, because they don't fit.

90
00:04:32,446 --> 00:04:34,584
Nobody would send this to their boss.

91
00:04:34,584 --> 00:04:35,912
So let's fix the looks.

92
00:04:36,612 --> 00:04:38,723
Quick detour for mistake number three.

93
00:04:38,723 --> 00:04:40,834
See index equals false in those lines?

94
00:04:40,834 --> 00:04:46,111
Leave it out, and pandas also writes its row numbers, zero, one, two, as an extra first column.

95
00:04:46,111 --> 00:04:49,500
Read the file back, and you get a column called Unnamed zero.

96
00:04:49,500 --> 00:04:52,777
It's harmless, but it confuses everyone who opens the file.

97
00:04:52,777 --> 00:04:56,888
Add index equals false, unless the index means something, like our months.

98
00:04:57,588 --> 00:04:59,558
Step six, formatting, with openpyxl.

99
00:04:59,558 --> 00:05:01,144
Import Font and Pattern Fill.

100
00:05:01,144 --> 00:05:04,264
Then, still inside the with block, loop over every sheet.

101
00:05:04,264 --> 00:05:06,288
Writer dot sheets gives you each one.

102
00:05:06,288 --> 00:05:11,378
For every cell in row one, the header, we set a bold white font, and a solid dark green fill.

103
00:05:11,378 --> 00:05:13,731
Then every column gets a width of fourteen.

104
00:05:13,731 --> 00:05:17,671
And freeze panes equals A two keeps the header visible while you scroll.

105
00:05:18,371 --> 00:05:23,067
Here's the difference on the By city sheet, the same data before and after.

106
00:05:23,067 --> 00:05:26,636
Wider columns, a real header, and a frozen line under it.

107
00:05:27,336 --> 00:05:28,843
Step seven, a totals row.

108
00:05:28,843 --> 00:05:31,316
Ws dot max row is the last row with data.

109
00:05:31,316 --> 00:05:36,442
One row below it, we write the word Total, and in column B, an Excel formula as text.

110
00:05:36,442 --> 00:05:38,191
Equals sum, B two to B eight.

111
00:05:38,191 --> 00:05:43,196
Because it's a real formula, if someone edits a number in Excel, the total updates.

112
00:05:43,196 --> 00:05:47,056
Then number format, with a comma for thousands and two decimals.

113
00:05:47,756 --> 00:05:52,011
Click the total cell, and the formula bar shows sum B two to B eight.

114
00:05:52,011 --> 00:05:54,477
A live Excel formula, written by Python.

115
00:05:54,477 --> 00:06:01,013
And the last two lines in the script give the Data sheet a proper date format, so the hash signs are gone.

116
00:06:01,713 --> 00:06:03,221
Here's the By month sheet.

117
00:06:03,221 --> 00:06:09,082
The months down the side, cities across, the green header, and it stays frozen while you scroll down.

118
00:06:09,782 --> 00:06:10,893
And the Data sheet.

119
00:06:10,893 --> 00:06:18,733
Every order, with real dates instead of hash signs, the new Revenue column at the end, with Month right after it, and a frozen header.

120
00:06:18,733 --> 00:06:22,185
Anyone can click a header in Excel and filter it from here.

121
00:06:22,885 --> 00:06:26,045
If your script fails, it's usually one of three things.

122
00:06:26,045 --> 00:06:31,102
No module named openpyxl means the library isn't installed, so run pip install openpyxl.

123
00:06:31,102 --> 00:06:36,044
A permission error on Windows usually means report dot x l s x is still open in Excel.

124
00:06:36,044 --> 00:06:37,365
Close it and run again.

125
00:06:37,365 --> 00:06:45,123
And a key error means a column name doesn't match, so check the spelling and the capital letters, exactly as they appear in the header.

126
00:06:45,823 --> 00:06:47,248
Here's the whole script.

127
00:06:47,248 --> 00:06:48,318
About forty lines.

128
00:06:48,318 --> 00:06:53,960
Load, filter, add two columns, two summaries, one Excel file, three sheets, and the formatting.

129
00:06:53,960 --> 00:06:55,980
Pause here if you want to copy it.

130
00:06:55,980 --> 00:06:58,653
It's also in the description, ready to paste.

131
00:06:59,353 --> 00:07:01,012
Always check what you made.

132
00:07:01,012 --> 00:07:07,771
This tiny script opens the report with load workbook, prints the sheet names, and prints every row of By city.

133
00:07:07,771 --> 00:07:11,642
Three sheets, seven cities, and the total row with its formula.

134
00:07:11,642 --> 00:07:12,994
Exactly what we built.

135
00:07:13,694 --> 00:07:14,124
Pro tip.

136
00:07:14,124 --> 00:07:19,458
Put the month in the file name, so every run makes a new report instead of overwriting the old one.

137
00:07:19,458 --> 00:07:20,912
From date time import date.

138
00:07:20,912 --> 00:07:25,545
Then an f string, report underscore, and date dot today with percent Y dash percent m.

139
00:07:25,545 --> 00:07:29,046
Today that gives report underscore twenty twenty six dash eleven.

140
00:07:29,046 --> 00:07:35,996
Combine it with episode twelve, where we scheduled a script to run by itself, and this report literally makes itself every month.

141
00:07:36,696 --> 00:07:37,505
And the speed?

142
00:07:37,505 --> 00:07:43,233
I timed the full script, reading the data, every summary, all three sheets, and all the formatting.

143
00:07:43,233 --> 00:07:44,390
Under half a second.

144
00:07:44,390 --> 00:07:48,267
The morning you used to spend on this report is now a coffee break.

145
00:07:48,967 --> 00:07:50,473
Here's your cheat sheet.

146
00:07:50,473 --> 00:07:52,167
Parse dates for real dates.

147
00:07:52,167 --> 00:07:53,924
A filter in square brackets.

148
00:07:53,924 --> 00:07:54,990
Group by and sum.

149
00:07:54,990 --> 00:07:55,743
Pivot table.

150
00:07:55,743 --> 00:07:57,563
Excel Writer for many sheets.

151
00:07:57,563 --> 00:07:58,755
Index equals false.

152
00:07:58,755 --> 00:08:01,955
And openpyxl for fonts, fills, widths and formulas.

153
00:08:01,955 --> 00:08:03,461
Pause and screenshot it.

154
00:08:04,161 --> 00:08:04,887
Quick recap.

155
00:08:04,887 --> 00:08:06,764
Read the file with parse dates.

156
00:08:06,764 --> 00:08:09,004
Filter, and add the columns you need.

157
00:08:09,004 --> 00:08:11,426
Summarize with group by and pivot table.

158
00:08:11,426 --> 00:08:14,151
Write all the sheets inside one Excel Writer.

159
00:08:14,151 --> 00:08:15,725
Then format with openpyxl.

160
00:08:15,725 --> 00:08:17,844
And put the month in the file name.

161
00:08:18,544 --> 00:08:26,666
Next on Shortcut Lab, we leave spreadsheets behind, and pull data straight from a web page, with your first web scraper in Python.

162
00:08:26,666 --> 00:08:28,665
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.
